    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Sidewalk is the third studio album by Australian rock band Icehouse, released in June 1984 on Chrysalis Records and Regular Records. The album reached #8 on the National albums chart and showcased a more somber and reflective direction for the band, with founding member Iva Davies prominently featuring the Fairlight Music Computer throughout the recording. The album produced three charting singles: \"Taking the Town\" (#22), \"Don't Believe Anymore\" (#31), and \"Dusty Pages\" (#82). Davies's distinctive vocals and songwriting prowess anchor the record, which blends new wave and synth-rock elements to create an eclectic sonic journey that captures the essence of 1980s music.\n\nThe album's sonic palette was significantly shaped by Davies's innovative use of the Fairlight synthesizer, which he customized through a close working relationship with Fairlight's engineers—modifications that were added to the standard design of this rare instrument, of which only 300 were ever built. Two tracks on Sidewalk were recorded in 1983 for director Russell Mulcahy's film Razorback, and shades of that soundtrack work permeate several songs on the album. The record features a stellar lineup including guitarist Robert Kretschmer, drummer John Lloyd, bassist Guy Pratt, and keyboardist Andy Qunta, with additional contributions from saxophonist Joe Camilleri.\n\nIn 2002, Warner Music digitally remastered Sidewalk and re-released it with four bonus tracks, introducing the album to a new generation. The album has endured as a classic in Icehouse's catalog, with \"Don't Believe Anymore\" becoming a fan favorite at the band's live performances. A 40th anniversary clear vinyl edition was released in 2024, cementing Sidewalk's status as a landmark recording that helped define the sound of 1980s alternative and electronic rock.",
